# The demand for financial assets

- The demand for foreign currency financial assets is relevant for the determination of the exchange rate.
- the demand for a generic financial asset depends on
	- yield
	- risk
	- liquidity 
- In the next few slides, we will introduce some notions for calculating the rate of return on financial assets in foreign currency.
- We will neglect risk and liquidity for the moment, considering stocks that have the same levels for these two variables.

# Refereeing: indirect quotation

- If A has an indirect quotation with respect to B
- the holders of A are better off buying B at a high exchange rate and 
- sell B at a low exchange rate.

example: A=\texteuro \ and B=\$; if $E_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}=$1.3 in London and $E_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}=$1.1 in NY, it is better to buy \$ in London and sell it in NY:

- Purchase of $1 million in London for 769231 texteuro
- sale of $1 million in NY in exchange for 909091 texteuro

Calculation of performance:

$$ \frac{909091-769231}{769231}=0.1818=\frac{1.3-1.1}{1.1} $$

# Refereeing: direct quotation

- If A has a direct quotation with respect to B
- holders of A are better off buying B at a low exchange rate and 
- sell B at a high exchange rate.

example: A=\$ \ and B=\texteuro; if $E_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}=$1.3 in London and $E_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}=$1.1 in NY, it is worth buying \texteuro \ in NY and selling them in London:

- Purchase of 1 million \texteuro\ in NY for \$1.1ml
- sale of 1 million \texteuro\ in London in exchange for \$1.3ml

$$ \frac{\$1.3ml-\$1.1ml}{\$1.1ml}=0.1818=\frac{1.3-1.1}{1.1} $$

# Time

If you buy/sell currency to sell/buy it in the future

- you know the spot exchange rate

$$E_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}$$

- Expectations about the future exchange rate must be formulated

$$E^e_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}$$

# Time: indirect quotation

The observations are similar to the arbitrage case:

- if A has a direct quotation with respect to B, it is better to buy B at a low exchange rate and sell it at a different exchange rate

- i.e., to buy B you should expect an increase in the exchange rate

- in a direct quotation an increase in the exchange rate is a depreciation of A with respect to B

# Time: direct quotation (example)

Example: A=\$ \ and B=\texteuro; If $E_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}=1.1$, it is worth buying euros to sell them in the future if $E^e_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}>1.1$.

Suppose $E^e_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}=1.3$

- Purchase 1 million \texteuro\ today for \$1.1ml
- Future sale of 1 million \texteuro\ in exchange for \$1.3ml

$$ \frac{\$1.3ml-\$1.1ml}{\$1.1ml}=0.1818=\frac{1.3-1.1}{1.1} $$

The return on the transaction is equal to the expected percentage depreciation of the exchange rate:

$$\frac{E^e_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}-E_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}}{E_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}}$$
 
# Time: indirect quotation

The observations are similar to the arbitrage case:

- if A has an indirect quotation compared to B, it is better to buy B at a high exchange rate and sell it at a low exchange rate

# Time: indirect quotation
Example: A=\texteuro \ and B=\$; If $E_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}=$1.3, it is worth buying dollars to sell them in the future if $E^e_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}<1.3$.

Suppose $E^e_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}=1.1$

- purchase of $1 million today for 769231 texteuro
- future sale of $1 million in exchange for 909091 texteuro

$$ \frac{909091-769231}{769231}=0.1818=\frac{1.3-1.1}{1.1} $$

The expected return of the operation is equal to the expected change in the exchange rate divided by the expected exchange rate, all changed in sign:

$$\frac{E_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}-E^e_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}}{E^e_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}}=-\frac{E^e_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}-E_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}}{E^e_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}}$$
 
# The yield on foreign securities (direct quotation)

Unlike buying and selling currency, buying and selling a foreign security yields an interest rate.

- an American pledges $E_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}$ dollars to get 1 euro and expects to get back $(1+R_{\textnormal{\texteuro}})E^e_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}$ dollars
- its expected return will therefore be
$$R_{\textnormal{ EU title in US}}=\frac{(1+R_{\textnormal{\texteuro}})E^e_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}-E_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}}{E_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}}$$
$$R_{\textnormal{ EU title in US}}=R_{\textnormal{\texteuro}}\frac{E^e_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}}{E_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}}+\frac{E^e_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}-E_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}}{E_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}}$$

# The yield on foreign securities (indirect quotation)

- A European pledges $\frac{1}{E_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}}$ euro to get $1 and expects to get $\frac{(1+R_{\textnormal{\$}})}{E^e_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}}$ euro
- its expected return will therefore be
$$R_{\textnormal{ US title in EU}}=\frac{\frac{(1+R_{\textnormal{\$}})}{E^e_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}}-\frac{1}{E_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}}}{\frac{1}{E_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}}}$$
$$R_{\textnormal{ US title in EU}}=R_{\textnormal{\$}}\frac{E_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}}{E^e_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}}-\frac{E^e_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}-E_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}}{E^e_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}}$$



# Approximations

The expressions we arrived at in the previous slides can be simplified as follows:

- For an American, the yield on a European bond is approximately equal to:
$$R_{\textnormal{ EU title in US}}=R_{\textnormal{\texteuro}}+\underbrace{\frac{E^e_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}-E_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}}{E_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}}}_{\textnormal{deprez. \$}}$$
- For a European, the yield on an American bond is approximately equal to:
$$R_{\textnormal{ US title in EU}}=R_{\textnormal{\$}}-\underbrace{\frac{E^e_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}-E_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}}{E_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}}}_{\textnormal{apprez. \texteuro}}$$

# The relationship between return in \$ and exchange rate

Let's be explicit with respect to the exchange rate:

\vskip5mm
$R_{\textnormal{EU title in US}}=R_{\textnormal{\texteuro}}+\frac{E^e_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}-E_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}}{E_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}}$

$R_{\textnormal{EU title in US}}\times E_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}=R_{\textnormal{\texteuro}}\times E_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}+E^e_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}-E_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}$

$R_{\textnormal{EU title in US}}\times E_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}-R_{\textnormal{\texteuro}}\times E_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}+E_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}=E^e_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}$

$(R_{\textnormal{EU title in US}}-R_{\textnormal{\texteuro}}+1)\times E_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}=E^e_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}$

\vskip4mm
$E_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}=\frac{E^e_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}}{R_{\textnormal{EU title in US}}+1-R_{\textnormal{\texteuro}}}$ \hskip2mm \footnotesize{graphically} \raisebox{-1cm}{\includegraphics[scale=0.4]{R_titolo_staniero.pdf}}


# The equilibrium exchange rate

The equilibrium is for $R_{\textnormal{EU stock in US}}=R_{\$}$

$$ E^*_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}=\frac{E^e_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}}{R_{\$}+1-R_{\textnormal{\texteuro}}}$$

It follows that:

- an increase of $E^e_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}$ causes an increase of $E^*_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}$
- an increase of $R_{\$}$ causes a reduction of $E^*_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}$
- An increase of $R_{\textnormal{\texteuro}}$ causes an increase of $E^*_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}$

Opposite conclusions are reached in the cases of reductions of $E^e_{\$/\textnormal{\texteuro}}$, $R_{\$}$ and $R_{\textnormal{\texteuro}}$.


